  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 250 AM Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Dangerous heat and humidity is expected today with \nmany areas across the southern and eastern portions of NC under a \nHeat Advisory. After a brief break in the dangerous heat in some \nareas, it returns Sunday and Monday.\n\nSatellite data indicated the pesky WNW flow aloft continues early \nthis morning from IL/IN ESE to off the mid-Atlantic coast. Strong to \nsevere storms were ongoing in the Midwest with plenty of mid and \nhigh cloudiness and even a few showers/storms extending southeast to \nthe VA/KY mountains. \n\nThis cloudiness is expected to slowly dissipate this morning with \nplenty of sun expected today. Strong heating with a very moist \nsurface layer that will not mix out as much today, will lead to \nhighs in the 90s. Dew points will be mostly in the mid 70s. This \nwill lead to heat indices topping out in the 105 to 109 range in the \nAdvisory area. Elsewhere, heat indices in the upper 90s to lower \n100s are expected in the NW Piedmont. Lows tonight will be in the \n70s, and if no storms are in the area - it will remain in the lower \nto mid 80s well into the night. \n\nThere may be a small break in the high heat and humidity on \nSaturday. However, this all depends on the storm chances later today \nand this evening and then again Saturday. The coverage Saturday may \nbe good enough to knock back the heat some - a bit like what just \noccurred late Wednesday into Thursday. Therefore, the storm threat \nmay be the main hazard Saturday. \n\nRegardless, the dangerous heat is expected to return Sunday and \nMonday as the upper ridge and SW flow at the surface return. The NBM \nappears a bit too hot on Monday with upper 90s to near 100 and a \nchance at 110 heat indices around RDU and FAY/RWI. It does have \nsupport from the EC ensemble, especially south and east of RDU.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A Marginal risk of severe thunderstorms this \nafternoon and evening. The main hazard is damaging wind gusts. \n\nAn MCV over the Midwest will likely graze NC to the north and east \nlater today in the problematic WNW flow aloft. Even so, strong \nheating today will allow for a corridor of MLCapes in excess of 2000 \nJ/kg as PW's increase to 2+ inches from WVA and VA across portions \nof northern NC. Scattered storms that develop upstream should be \nable to continue strong to locally severe (with wind damage the \nprimary threat) as they track into NC. The timing is a bit of a \nchallenge but the latest CAMS suggest mid to late afternoon in the \nwest and late day into the evening in the north and east. This will \nbe adjusted with analysis today. A few wet microbursts are possible. \n\nKey Message 3... A Marginal to Slight risk of severe thunderstorms \nSaturday with the higher risk in the western areas of central NC.\n\nStrong instability is expected to develop with high PW's with \nsurface heating into the 90s. Scattered storms that develop over the \nmountains of VA/NC/WVA will again likely surge SE into NC. A few wet \nmicrobursts will be possible along with potentially more coverage \nthan today. The main timing would be mid to late afternoon in the NW \nand elsewhere late afternoon into the evening.",
